## Summary

| Step | Action | Finding |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `ping` | Host is up |
| 2 | `nmap -sV` | Port 21 (FTP) open |
| 3 | `ftp` login as `anonymous` | Unauthenticated access granted |
| 4 | `ls` / `get flag.txt` | Flag downloaded and read |

## Takeaway

FTP was never designed with confidentiality in mind — by default it transmits everything, including credentials, in plaintext, making it vulnerable to interception via Man-in-the-Middle attacks unless it's wrapped in SSL/TLS (FTPS) or tunneled through SSH (SFTP). On top of that, anonymous login being left enabled turns the service into an open file share for anyone who finds it. This box is a clean example of why FTP should either be disabled in favor of SFTP, or at minimum have anonymous access explicitly turned off and credentials enforced.